from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "9. The Foundation of the Apostles and Prophets"),
("p", "Claim: the household of God is “built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone” (Ephesians 2:20). The prophets of that verse and of Ephesians 3:5 are prophets of the Mystery, to whom the secret was revealed now. They are not the Twelve, not the prophets of the Old Testament, and not the prophets of Israel’s Kingdom. The foundation is the revelation of the mystery, laid once, and no verse names a successor. This chapter argues each part from the passages."),
("sub", "9.1 The verse and its setting"),
("p", "Paul writes to the Gentiles of Ephesus: “Now therefore ye are no more strangers and foreigners, but fellowcitizens with the saints, and of the household of God; and are built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone; in whom all the building fitly framed together groweth unto an holy temple in the Lord: in whom ye also are builded together for an habitation of God through the Spirit” (Ephesians 2:19–22). The figure is a building. The foundation is laid, the corner stone is Christ, and the building grows."),
("p", "Three things are stated. The foundation belongs to the apostles and prophets. Jesus Christ himself is the chief corner stone. And the building is still growing, because it “groweth unto an holy temple.” A foundation is laid at the start of a building and not at its end. The verse speaks of a foundation that has been laid, and of a structure that rises on it."),
("sub", "9.2 Who the prophets are: the word order"),
("p", "The verse says “apostles and prophets,” and the same order stands in the two other places where the pair is named in this letter: “his holy apostles and prophets by the Spirit” (Ephesians 3:5) and “he gave some, apostles; and some, prophets” (Ephesians 4:11). It stands in the order of the gifts to the Body in 1 Corinthians 12:28, “first apostles, secondarily prophets.” The Lord’s words recorded by Luke have the reverse order, and they are about Israel: “I will send them prophets and apostles, and some of them they shall slay and persecute” (Luke 11:49). There the prophets come first, because the Lord is speaking of the line of prophets sent to Israel, and the apostles who are sent after them."),
("p", "The order is a mark, not a proof. It shows that Ephesians and 1 Corinthians keep one pairing, with the apostles first, and that Luke 11:49 keeps another. The volume uses it as one line of evidence beside the others."),
("sub", "9.3 Who the prophets are: “now revealed”"),
("p", "The decisive passage is Ephesians 3. Paul writes of the mystery: “by revelation he made known unto me the mystery; … Which in other ages was not made known unto the sons of men, as it is now revealed unto his holy apostles and prophets by the Spirit; that the Gentiles should be fellowheirs, and of the same body, and partakers of his promise in Christ by the gospel” (Ephesians 3:3–6). He adds that the mystery “from the beginning of the world hath been hid in God, who created all things by Jesus Christ” (Ephesians 3:9)."),
("p", "The verse draws a line between “other ages” and “now.” In other ages the mystery was not made known to the sons of men; now it is revealed to his holy apostles and prophets by the Spirit. The prophets named here are therefore those to whom the mystery is revealed now. They cannot be the prophets of the Old Testament, to whom, by the verse’s own statement, the mystery was not made known, and who are the prophets of other ages. They are not the Twelve, who are called apostles in the Gospels and whose commission, in Matthew 10:5, 6, was to the house of Israel. And they are not the prophets of Israel’s Kingdom, whose word concerns the kingdom promised to Israel and not the mystery hid in God."),
("p", "The prophets of Ephesians 2:20 and 3:5 are prophets of the Mystery. They are the prophets in the assemblies of the overlap to whom the secret was revealed, as Paul states it, now. The volume does not name them. The persons of chapter 8 are persons of the assemblies, and the passages do not say which of them were of this company. The volume says only what the verse says: the mystery is revealed to them by the Spirit."),
("tbl", "The prophets of Ephesians 3:5.", [
    ("Ephesians 3:3–5", T("Eph 3:3-5"), "The mystery was not made known in other ages; it is now revealed to his holy apostles and prophets by the Spirit."),
    ("Ephesians 3:9", T("Eph 3:9"), "The mystery from the beginning of the world hath been hid in God."),
    ("Luke 11:49", T("Luke 11:49"), "The Lord speaks of prophets and apostles sent to Israel, with the prophets first."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "9.4 The foundation laid once"),
("p", "Paul uses the same figure to the Corinthians and puts the work in the past tense: “According to the grace of God which is given unto me, as a wise masterbuilder, I have laid the foundation, and another buildeth thereon. But let every man take heed how he buildeth thereupon. For other foundation can no man lay than that is laid, which is Jesus Christ” (1 Corinthians 3:10, 11)."),
("p", "Three statements stand in the passage. Paul has laid the foundation, and the verb is past. Another buildeth thereon, and the builders are told to take heed. And no man can lay another foundation than the one that is laid, which is Jesus Christ. The passage gives a pattern of one laying and many buildings. It does not speak of a second laying or of foundation-layers who come after. In Ephesians the foundation is the apostles and prophets, with Christ the corner stone, and in 1 Corinthians it is Christ himself. The two are not set against each other: the revelation of the mystery is the revelation of Christ, and Christ is the stone in which the structure is fitly framed together."),
("p", "The volume reads the foundation as the revelation of the mystery, laid once, by the apostles and prophets to whom it was made known. The reading rests on three things the passages state: the foundation is laid, it is the foundation of the apostles and prophets, and the mystery is what was revealed to them. It does not rest on the claim that no other man could preach or teach, because other men build on the foundation and are told to take heed how."),
("sub", "9.5 No successor named"),
("p", "The passages that name the foundation name no successor to the apostles or the prophets. Paul’s letters to Timothy and Titus, which give directions for the assemblies, do not tell either man to appoint an apostle or a prophet. They tell Titus to “ordain elders in every city” (Titus 1:5) and speak of the “office of a bishop” (1 Timothy 3:1). The directions concern elders and overseers, and they do not mention apostles or prophets as offices to be filled. The argument from silence is plain here: the occasion for naming a successor is present, and none is named."),
("p", "The volume says no more than that. It does not say that no man after Paul spoke by the Spirit, and it does not say that the elders and overseers of the assemblies held a lesser calling than the foundation-layers. It says that the passages which give the structure of the assembly do not give an apostle or a prophet as an office to be appointed, and that the foundation is spoken of as laid."),
("sub", "9.6 A word on Matthew 16:18"),
("p", "The Lord’s words to Peter, “upon this rock I will build my church” (Matthew 16:18), are sometimes brought into the question of the foundation. The verse is read in its own chapter, where the Lord is speaking to the Twelve in the days of his ministry in the land. The volume reports it and does not argue it, because it falls under the Gospel Commentary Series and the Acts Commentary Series. The argument of this chapter does not depend on it."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Ephesians 2:20 speaks of a foundation of the apostles and prophets laid at the start of a building that still grows.",
    "The prophets of Ephesians 2:20 and 3:5 are those to whom the mystery is now revealed. They are not the Twelve, not the prophets of the Old Testament and not the prophets of Israel’s Kingdom.",
    "1 Corinthians 3:10, 11 puts the laying of the foundation in the past and names Jesus Christ as the foundation that is laid.",
    "The letters that give directions for the assemblies name elders and overseers and name no successor to an apostle or a prophet.",
  ]),
]
